# {{ inventory_hostname }}: operations manual

Generated by Ansible (remote-exit-node). It contains WireGuard **private keys**: keep it in a
password manager only, never in git, email or chat. Re-run the playbook after any change
(e.g. after the box moves) to refresh it.

Not in this document, store separately: the **vault password**, the node user's **sudo
password**, the **SSH private key** (`{{ ansible_ssh_private_key_file | default('~/.ssh/...') }}`) and the repo's `.env`.

## 1. At a glance

| Item | Value |
|---|---|
| Node name | `{{ inventory_hostname }}` |
| Login user | `{{ ssh_user }}` |
| Hardware MAC ({{ node_wan_interface }}) | `{{ lan['macaddress'] }}` |
| LAN IP when generated | `{{ lan['address'] }}` |
| LAN gateway (router) when generated | `{{ lan['gateway'] }}` |
| Timezone | `{{ node_timezone }}` |
| Tailscale | {{ ('`' ~ tailscale_hostname ~ '` / `' ~ ts_ip ~ '`') if enable_tailscale | bool else 'disabled' }} |
| DuckDNS | {{ ('`' ~ duckdns_domain ~ '.duckdns.org`') if enable_duckdns | bool else 'disabled' }} |
| WireGuard endpoint | {{ ('`' ~ wireguard_endpoint ~ ':' ~ wireguard_port ~ '` (UDP)') if wg_on else 'disabled' }} |
| Node inside WireGuard | {{ ('`' ~ wireguard_ipv4_server ~ '`') if wg_on else 'n/a' }} |
| Heartbeat monitoring | {{ 'configured' if monitoring_heartbeat_url | length > 0 else 'not configured' }} |

## 2. How to log in

Two independent paths. If one is down, use the other.

**A. Tailscale (primary, works through any NAT)**

```bash
ssh {{ ssh_user }}@{{ tailscale_hostname }}
```

Tailscale SSH may ask you to confirm in a browser (re-checked every 12 h).

{% if wg_on %}
**B. WireGuard + SSH (backup, independent of Tailscale)**

1. Connect one of the WireGuard clients from section 5 (keep one, e.g. `{{ wireguard_peers[-1].name }}`, on your laptop as the emergency key).
2. Then:

```bash
ssh -i {{ ansible_ssh_private_key_file | default('~/.ssh/<key>') }} {{ ssh_user }}@{{ wireguard_ipv4_server }}
```

Needs the router port forward from section 3. `{{ wireguard_ipv4_server }}` is fixed; it never changes.

{% endif %}
**C. On site**: keyboard + monitor on the box, log in as `{{ ssh_user }}` with the sudo password.

**Reach the remote router's admin page** (e.g. to fix a port forward), from either path:

```bash
ssh -L 8080:{{ lan['gateway'] }}:80 {{ ssh_user }}@{{ tailscale_hostname if enable_tailscale | bool else wireguard_ipv4_server }}
# then open http://localhost:8080 (use :443 and https:// if the router requires it)
```

Update the router IP if the remote network uses a different one (see `ip route` on the node).

No Tailscale subnet route is needed for this. The SSH tunnel uses the box as a hop only
while it is open, so the remote LAN is never exposed to your tailnet, nothing has to be
approved in the Tailscale console, and it also works over the WireGuard path. Same trick for
any other device on the remote LAN: `ssh -L <local-port>:<device-ip>:<port> ...`.

## 3. Remote router setup (at the remote site)

{% if wg_on %}
| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| DHCP reservation | MAC `{{ lan['macaddress'] }}` → a fixed LAN IP, e.g. the current one |
| Port forward | **UDP {{ wireguard_port }}** (external) → that fixed IP, port **{{ wireguard_port }}** |
| Nothing else | No TCP ports, no DMZ, no UPnP needed |

Without the port forward only Tailscale works. Verify it with `scripts/doctor.sh` from a
different network (section 6). If it fails although the forward is set: compare the router's
WAN IP with `curl -4 ifconfig.me` on the remote network. Different means CGNAT (WireGuard
can't work there) or a second router in front (forward the port on both).
{% else %}
WireGuard is disabled: no port forwarding needed. A DHCP reservation for MAC
`{{ lan['macaddress'] }}` is still recommended.
{% endif %}

## 4. For the person at the remote site

- The box must stay plugged into power and the router (network cable).
- It starts by itself after a power cut.
- If the VPN stops working: **unplug the power, wait 10 seconds, plug it back in**, wait 3 minutes.
- Please don't reset the router; if the router is replaced, the port forward from section 3 must be set up again.

{% if wg_on %}
## 5. WireGuard clients

Each device gets its **own** config; don't use one config on two devices at the same time.
QR codes for phones: `{{ artifacts_dir }}/<name>.png` (attach them next to this document).

UniFi gateway: Settings → VPN → VPN Client → Create New → WireGuard → upload the config
(or paste it). Then Settings → Routing → Traffic Routes to choose which devices/domains use it.
If the host's IP changes and the UniFi tunnel stays down, toggle the VPN client off and on:
WireGuard looks up `{{ wireguard_endpoint }}` only when the tunnel starts.
{% if wireguard_peers | selectattr('server_keepalive', 'defined') | list | length > 0 %}
Server keepalive is on for: {{ wireguard_peers | selectattr('server_keepalive', 'defined') | map(attribute='name') | join(', ') }} (may let them recover by themselves).
{% endif %}
For the always-on router client, add `server_keepalive: 25` and `expect_connected: true` to its
entry in `wireguard_peers`; the heartbeat then alerts when that tunnel is down.

